-- #1036 (S0 silent wrongness): two impls whose types share a HEAD TYCON but differ in
-- TYPE ARGUMENTS (`Box Int` / `Box String`), one overriding and one inheriting the
-- interface default.  The native binary dispatched the INHERITING type into the
-- OVERRIDING type's impl: `useSpeak (Box "s")` printed "boxint", exit 0, no diagnostic.
--
-- Two blind spots, one bug:
--
--   * `defaultReachesOtherTags` / the mixed default+impls chain diffed HEAD TAGS.
--     `Box String`'s head is "Box", which the `Box Int` entry already covers, so
--     nothing looked uncovered — `soleImplDirect` took the sole-impl shortcut and
--     compiled every dict-routed `speak` into `call @mdk_impl_Box_speak`, never
--     reading the dict.
--
--   * even without that shortcut no arm could have fired: the emitter derived this
--     impl's route key from `headTagUnique` over CImplEntries — and the inheriting
--     impl produces NONE — so it hashed the bare head "Box", while typecheck's
--     INTERFACE-keyed `keyForSiteByIface` (which does see a method-less impl) stamped
--     the canonical key `Speak|(Box Int)|` into the caller's dict cell.  Different
--     words for the same impl.
--
-- The CONSTRAINED FORWARDER is required: a direct `speak (Box 1)` resolves RKey and
-- takes emitDefaultRKey, which was always correct.
--
-- Speak  — override declared FIRST, two inheriting siblings after it.
-- Loud   — the inheriting sibling declared FIRST (arm/dedup ORDER is load-bearing:
--          `ifaceTags` unions decl-derived keys ahead of entry-derived ones and
--          `dedupS` keeps the LAST occurrence).
-- Both   — the CONTROL: both siblings OVERRIDE, so C7 already keyed them correctly
--          before this fix.  It must still print bi/bs.
--
-- Described — the NON-TRIVIAL DEFAULT.  `Cat` overrides `describe`; `Dog` INHERITS it,
--          and the inherited body is not a constant: it calls two SIBLING methods on
--          its own receiver and does arithmetic.  A right-arm/wrong-body synthesis, or
--          sibling calls re-stamped to the other impl, changes the printed string —
--          which a constant-string default cannot show.
--
-- Must print:  boxint / woof / woof / BOXINT / WOOF / bi / bs
--              / OVERRIDDEN-cat / dog/71
import tface.{
  Speak(..),
  speak,
  Loud(..),
  loud,
  Both(..),
  both,
  Described(..),
  describe,
}

data Box a = Box a

impl Speak (Box Int) where
  speak _ = "boxint"

-- INHERITS `speak`: same head tycon "Box", different type argument.
impl Speak (Box String) where


-- A SECOND inheriting sibling — the chain needs one default arm per uncovered key,
-- not just one.
impl Speak (Box Bool) where


-- INHERITS `loud`, and is declared BEFORE the override.
impl Loud (Box String) where


impl Loud (Box Int) where
  loud _ = "BOXINT"

-- CONTROL: neither inherits.
impl Both (Box Int) where
  both _ = "bi"

impl Both (Box String) where
  both _ = "bs"

-- Described: `Cat` OVERRIDES describe, `Dog` INHERITS the NON-CONSTANT default, whose
-- body calls two siblings on its own receiver and does arithmetic.  Cat's and Dog's
-- sibling values differ, so a default whose sibling calls were stamped to the wrong
-- receiver prints `cat/31` instead of `dog/71`, and dropped arithmetic prints a
-- different number.  DISTINCT head tycons on purpose — see tface.mdk.
data Cat = Cat
data Dog = Dog

impl Described Cat where
  tagOf _ = "cat"
  sizeOf _ = 3
  describe _ = "OVERRIDDEN-cat"

impl Described Dog where
  tagOf _ = "dog"
  sizeOf _ = 7

useSpeak : Speak a => a -> String
useSpeak x = speak x

useDescribed : Described a => a -> String
useDescribed x = describe x

useLoud : Loud a => a -> String
useLoud x = loud x

useBoth : Both a => a -> String
useBoth x = both x

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = putStrLn (useSpeak (Box 1))
  let _ = putStrLn (useSpeak (Box "s"))
  let _ = putStrLn (useSpeak (Box True))
  let _ = putStrLn (useLoud (Box 2))
  let _ = putStrLn (useLoud (Box "t"))
  let _ = putStrLn (useBoth (Box 3))
  let _ = putStrLn (useBoth (Box "u"))
  let _ = putStrLn (useDescribed Cat)
  putStrLn (useDescribed Dog)
